<title>USA: SHOWERS BOOST MONTANA SOIL MOISTURE IN WEEK.</title>
<headline>SHOWERS BOOST MONTANA SOIL MOISTURE IN WEEK.</headline>
<dateline>HELENA, Montana 1997-08-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop summary from the Montana Agricultural Statistics Service for the week ending Sunday, August 10.</p>
<p>CROPS: Although temperatures remained high for the majority of the state, frequent rain showers improved topsoil and subsoil conditions as well as crops.</p>
<p>The weekend brought cooler temperatures and showers to most of the state.</p>
<p>Topsoil moisture is rated at 9 percent very short, 41 percent short, 49 percent adequate, and 1 percent surplus, and subsoil moisture numbers were 9 percent very short, 38 percent short, 52 percent adequate, and 1 percent surplus.</p>
<p>Montana farmers had 6.4 days suitable for field work although rains slowed the harvest in some places.  Hail storms continue to cause some damage.  Winter wheat harvest has progressed to 61 percent complete. Barley and oat harvests are in full swing with barley 27 percent ripe and 10 percent harvested and oats 29 percent ripe and 5 percent harvested.  Spring wheat is now 92 percent turned and 19 percent ripe and harvest has started with 4 percent of the acreage harvested.  Grasshoppers and wheat stem sawfly continue to plague farmers in parts of the state.</p>
<p>Haying is progressing slowly due to localized rains.  Reporters are indicating that haying of CRP land is almost complete.  The first cutting of alfalfa is complete, and the second cutting of alfalfa is 31 percent complete.  Other hay harvest has increased to 89 percent complete.</p>
